\paragraph{Values of the search settings.}
% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION
Before the full runs, we ran a series of preliminary design experiments to determine the values each setting of the search may take. They used 1 to 9 datasets and 1 to 5 folds, and each dataset was scored in its own metric, with scores never averaged across datasets. When making a selection, we considered whether a value gave the best score for some dataset, downstream model and budget, as well as the compute time in hours. A value that won somewhere but cost many hours stayed in the full search and was left out of the fast tier. Each setting's values were then either kept, because each won for some dataset, model and budget, or fixed at one value. A setting was fixed when the spread between its values was smaller than the spread of one value across folds. As an example of a kept value, nine downstream models were fitted on each of 10 base representations over 5 folds. The datasets were Buchwald--Hartwig yield, Caco-2 efflux, Koenig expression, LogD and breast cancer PAM50 subtype. The DNN scored best on 4 of the 5 datasets and third on Caco-2 (0.2936 against 0.2851 RMSE, log$_{10}$ efflux ratio), so it was added. As an example of a fixed rule, we tested five ways of rescaling each representation's score before the budget is split. On hERG $K_i$, with linear allocation at 300 features, the five rescalings gave RMSE values from 0.5870 to 0.5924 (p$K_i$). A single rescaling, however, ranged from 0.5475 to 0.6294 RMSE across the 5 folds, so rescaling is one fixed rule per metric. Every design experiment is listed in Table~\ref{tab:add:design_experiments}.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{Importance methods.}
% CODE INPUT
Two comparisons were run, the first of how the methods rank features and the second of the hybrids those rankings build. The ranking comparison scored 8 importance methods on 7 datasets, namely LogD, hERG $K_i$, Buchwald--Hartwig yield, nanobody thermostability, antibody FcRn retention, breast invasive carcinoma (BRCA) PAM50 subtype and lung adenocarcinoma (LUAD) vs lung squamous cell carcinoma (LUSC). For each pair of methods, it measured the Spearman correlation between their scores over every feature of every representation. The three retained methods rank features differently. Across the 7 datasets, the correlation ranged from 0.20 to 0.64 between LightGBM gain and TreeSHAP, from 0.21 to 0.67 between LightGBM gain and DeepLIFT, and from 0.56 to 0.99 between TreeSHAP and DeepLIFT. These numbers are preliminary, because each method was fitted once on the whole dataset rather than inside folds.

% CODE INPUT
The hybrid comparison used Caco-2, with 2,158 molecules, 10 representations, 3 scaffold folds and a budget of 100 features. Hybrids were built from each method's ranking and scored with random forest and LightGBM downstream models. Three control rankings were added, namely feature variance, Laplacian score and a random order. The measure was the RMSE of the best single representation minus the RMSE of the hybrid, in log$_{10}$ efflux ratio units, so that a positive value means the hybrid has the lower error. The preliminary results were $+0.0144$ for DeepLIFT, $+0.0100$ for LightGBM gain, $+0.0067$ for TreeSHAP and $-0.0118$ for the random ranking. The gap between DeepLIFT and LightGBM gain, 0.0044 RMSE, is smaller than the fold-to-fold spread. The methods also differ at the top of their rankings. Of each representation's 100 top-ranked features on Caco-2, the retained pairs of methods share a median of 28 to 54 features.

% CODE INPUT
Three rules determined which methods were retained. First, a method must score every feature of every representation for regression, binary and multiclass labels, which all three retained methods do. Second, of two methods whose rankings agree above a Spearman correlation of 0.95, we keep the faster one. Under this rule, DeepLIFT replaced integrated gradients, which agreed with it above 0.95 on 4 of 6 datasets, and gradient SHAP, which agreed above 0.95 on 3 of 6. Third, we keep methods that rank features differently, because a different ranking builds a different hybrid. TreeSHAP came last of the three on Caco-2 and was kept under this third rule. Only two filters act on features before allocation, the cut at a score of 0 and the pre-screen on representations wider than 2,000 features. On a 2,048-bit fingerprint, the pre-screen drops the 48 lowest-variance bits. On hERG $K_i$ all 48 are among the 105 constant ECFP4 bits, while on QM9, which has no constant ECFP4 bits, they are near-constant ones. Near-constant features otherwise remain candidates and are ranked by importance.

% CODE INPUT
No single importance method suits every dataset and downstream model. A preliminary run used 5 datasets (nanobody thermostability, hERG $K_i$, humanness, LogD and BRCA PAM50 subtype), 5 folds, 10 representations, 5 downstream models and budgets of 25, 50, 100, 150 and 300 features. For each of the 25 pairs of downstream model and budget per dataset, we counted which importance method gave the best configuration, and Table~\ref{tab:importance_wins} gives the counts. LightGBM gain won the most pairs on three datasets and tied with TreeSHAP on humanness. DeepLIFT won the most on one dataset, LogD, with 17 of its 25 pairs, and outside LogD it gave the best configuration for 2 of 100 pairs. With the DNN downstream model, LightGBM gain won 17 of 20 budgets outside LogD, whereas on LogD the DNN took DeepLIFT at 3 of 5 budgets. On LogD, XGBoost and LightGBM each took DeepLIFT at 5 of 5 budgets, and random forest took LightGBM gain at 5 of 5. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: the same win counts, per dataset and importance method, from the full runs]}

