\paragraph{Matched internal evaluator.}
% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% CODE INPUT
Matching the evaluator to the downstream model is the obvious default. However, the evaluator only has to rank representations, and another model may rank them better for the downstream model. Because the matched evaluator is one of the four searched values, the search already covers this default, and a mismatched evaluator winning is informative rather than a negative result. We first counted wins in the search itself, in a preliminary run on 5 datasets (nanobody thermostability, hERG $K_i$, humanness, LogD and breast cancer PAM50 subtype). These runs used 10 representations, budgets of 25 to 300 features and 5 folds, and their scores are the search's own fold scores rather than held-out scores. The matched evaluator gave the best score for its downstream model in 9 of 25 pairs of dataset and downstream model. Counted per budget, it was best in 43 of 125 combinations of dataset, downstream model and budget. With 3 or 4 evaluators per downstream model and no effect of matching, about 38 of 125 would be expected. On nanobody thermostability with a DNN downstream model, the matched evaluator reached a Spearman correlation of 0.6696 against 0.6506 for the LightGBM evaluator, a gap of 0.0190. Its 5 fold scores ranged from 0.5095 to 0.7920. On LogD with an SVM downstream model, the matched evaluator scored 0.4322 RMSE (log units) against 0.4217 for the LightGBM evaluator, where lower is better. This is a gap of 0.0105, the matched evaluator lost at all 5 budgets, and its 5 fold scores ranged from 0.4118 to 0.4634. We then scored the evaluators on held-out folds in a second preliminary run, with 3 outer folds, a budget of 250 features, 5 representations and LightGBM gain only. The downstream models were random forest and SVM. On nanobody thermostability with a random forest downstream model, the matched evaluator reached a Spearman correlation of 0.6098, the LightGBM evaluator 0.6220 and the SVM evaluator 0.6172. The matched evaluator's fold scores ranged from 0.5659 to 0.6734. With an SVM downstream model, the matched evaluator reached 0.6189, the Gaussian process evaluator 0.6265 and the LightGBM evaluator 0.6317, with fold scores for the matched evaluator from 0.5530 to 0.6703. On hERG $K_i$ with a random forest downstream model, the matched evaluator scored 0.6046 RMSE (p$K_i$), the SVM evaluator 0.6059 and the LightGBM evaluator 0.6075. Its fold scores ranged from 0.5957 to 0.6178, and it beat the SVM evaluator on 2 of 3 folds. With an SVM downstream model, the matched evaluator scored 0.5723 RMSE (p$K_i$), the LightGBM evaluator 0.5724 and the Gaussian process evaluator 0.5765, with fold scores for the matched evaluator from 0.5605 to 0.5862. Every gap above, both held out and within the search, is smaller than the range of the matched evaluator's own fold scores. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: per dataset and downstream model, how often the winning configuration used the matched evaluator, and its gap to the best other evaluator in the dataset's metric]}

