\paragraph{hERG blockers held at separate sites.} The separate-site experiments of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} use two further hERG sets from Lhasa Limited \citep{Bassani2023, Hanser2023}: a training set of 7,772 molecules (3,630 blockers, 46.7\%) and a test set of 4,383 molecules (1,084 blockers). Each label is Lhasa's own binary blocker call, made under different rules in the two sets. Both are proprietary (Additional file~\ref{app:availability}). The hERG $K_i$ set acts as a third site, with a compound counted as a blocker when $\mathrm{p}K_i \ge 6.0$, giving 467 blockers and 945 non-blockers. Molecules are matched by InChIKey across the sites, in the order hERG $K_i$, training set, test set, keeping the first copy. This removes 166 training and 170 test molecules, all of them also in the hERG $K_i$ set; the two Lhasa sets share none.

