\subsection{Cancer Genomics (TCGA)}\label{app:datasets:tcga}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% TODO: review, needs manual review
The two cancer datasets use TCGA data \citep{Weinstein2013} from UCSC Xena \citep{Goldman2020}. Expression is each cohort's Illumina HiSeq RNA-seq matrix on the Xena TCGA hub (version 2017-10-13): the same 20,530 genes in every cohort, unfiltered, as $\log_2(x+1)$ of the RSEM-normalized count \citep{Li2011RSEM}. Only primary tumors are kept, one sample per patient. No batch correction is applied, because every fold holds out whole tissue source sites and a correction fitted on the training sites cannot adjust an unseen site.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{Lung adenocarcinoma against lung squamous cell carcinoma.} The adenocarcinoma \citep{TCGA2014LUAD} and squamous cell carcinoma \citep{TCGA2012LUSC} cohorts are stacked, with the cohort as the label. Dropping 110 normal-tissue and 2 recurrent-tumor samples leaves 515 adenocarcinomas and 502 squamous tumors, 1,017 in all, from 68 tissue source sites.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% TODO: review, needs manual review
\paragraph{Breast cancer PAM50 subtype.} The label is the PAM50 subtype call \citep{Parker2009} from the Xena breast cancer clinical matrix \citep{TCGA2012BRCA}. Tumors called normal-like or without a call are dropped, leaving 821 of 1,097 primary tumors with expression data: 421 luminal A, 192 luminal B, 141 basal-like and 67 HER2-enriched, from 19 tissue source sites.
